@HeathieJ  We believe a good way to think about this story is to try to not to make value judgments about its elements but rather to see it as an exposition of "the ways things are", or at least tend to be.  And we believe it does that brilliantly.

People and things use other people and things, and that means people and things get used.  And we always have a choice as to what attitude we will take towards however we fall within that difficult reality.  And surely if we are being honest with ourselves we will realize we are almost always falling on both sides.

And of course, the reader (or listener and looker in the case of a child being read to) can decide how they will attempt to live with, or perhaps improve on, that difficult reality.  The beauty of it is that even though it is a "children's book", it can have a profound effect on an adult reader.

