@Lynn And in re your other points, as we were saying yesterday we took 15 years off from regular solving and when we took it up again all of a sudden we were better solvers.  That flies in the face of "practice makes perfect" so we have been attributing to easier puzzles.  And of course that idea gets lots of confirmation here.

But you have us thinking - we also realize that we're much more strategic solvers now,and maybe that's owing to 15 years more of life experience.  Before it was sort of either we recognized an answer or not.  Now if we don't recognize an answer we try to imagine the possibilities more and reason through which is most likely.

And in re your comment about eras, that approach may be well suited to today's prevalence of multi-word "conversational" answers.  Those are easier for us to reason through than a proper noun or a single "vocabulary" word-type answer.  And if that can be done there is a high "crossings yield".

@Down_Home
I can understand that. I think that is why I used to do a lot of research outside the puzzle, to find names in some way that didn't involve doing a search on the clue itself. At this point I have developed strategies that usually enable me to deal with those situations without going outside the puzzle, but I think they take longer than the research I used to do. Which might be one thing that keeps my averages from going down. 

Unsatisfying for me now is if I get a puzzle done without even reading a bunch of the down clues. Because I like working the crosses, as Rich described. That has not happened as much for weekend puzzles, but it did once recently.

@Down_Home
Hmm, and now rereading Peaty & Oaty's reply above (a couple of levels down, if you can find it), they make a point relevant to both yours and mine. They point out that reasoning through unfamiliar answers rather than doing research is a strategy that might be particularly well suited to "multi-word 'conversational' answers" as opposed to unfamiliar names that cross. 

"Those are easier for us to reason through than a proper noun or a single 'vocabulary' word-type answer.  And if that can be done there is a high "crossings yield." - Peaty & Oaty

